// Copilot review (PR #2515), round-3 comment 4113834074:
// `checker_check_expr_call_inplace`'s `Type::method` associated-function
// arm (self-hosted/check/check.sio) resolves a 2-segment path's penultimate
// segment through the GLOBAL struct/enum tables via `fn_sig_table_find_
// method_semantic` before ever asking whether that segment is a module the
// CURRENT caller actually `use`d. `zz` here names both: the real module
// `zz.sio` (via `use zz;` below, exporting `f() -> i64 { 42 }`) AND this
// file's own unrelated lowercase `struct zz` with its own method `f`.
// Before the round-3 fix, `zz::f()` always bound the struct's method
// regardless of the real module import.
//
// Copilot review (PR #2515), comment 4114680413: the round-3 fix instead
// made the checker prefer the module unconditionally -- but the LOWERER's
// equivalent lookup (self-hosted/ir/lower.sio's callee_path_module_
// stripped_name / LOWER_IMPL_METHOD_HASH) has no such asymmetric signal
// and can still pick the struct's method body, so the accepted program
// segfaults at runtime (confirmed directly). A --check pass the compiler
// then crashes on is worse than a refusal, so the checker now REFUSES this
// exact ambiguous shape (E263) instead of guessing either way. Closing
// this for real -- accepting AND running it correctly -- needs the shared
// "qualifier -> target-module-id" resolution table already documented
// elsewhere in this PR as a genuine architectural addition; see this
// fixture's own README.
use zz;

struct zz {
    n: i64,
}

impl zz {
    fn f(self) -> i64 with Mut, Panic, Div, Alloc {
        self.n + 1000
    }
}

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Panic {
    let v: i64 = zz::f()
    if v == 42 {
        println("MODULE_QUALIFIER_WINS_OVER_TYPE_COLLISION PASS")
        return 0
    }
    1
}
